--TEST--
`[...$x]` checks the spread subject against `array<T>` for the literal's own `T` — `rule:types/arrays`, and `E0484` where the position names no `array<T>` to check against at all
--FILE--
<?nvs
array<string> $a = ["x"];
array<int> $n = [1];
string $s = "s";
// Accepted, and pinned by the absence of a diagnostic on this line: an
// array read is element-covariant, and a spread is a read, so `array<string>`
// contributes to an `array<mixed>` exactly as an element of it would.
array<mixed> $wide = [...$a];
// The subject is checked against `array<T>` for the literal's own `T`, so a
// mismatch names both array types rather than the element alone.
array<string> $rows = [...$n];
// Not an array at all, in a position that does name an element type.
array<string> $chars = [...$s];
// The same mistake where the position names no `array<T>` to check against:
// `mixed` accepts the literal, so nothing but this code refuses the spread.
mixed $any = [...$s];
echo "unreached\n";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `array<string>`, found `array<int>`
   --> %s:11:27
   |
11 | array<string> $rows = [...$n];
   |                           ^^ this is `array<int>`

error[E0401]: expected `array<string>`, found `string`
   --> %s:13:28
   |
13 | array<string> $chars = [...$s];
   |                            ^^ this is `string`

error[E0484]: a `...` array element needs an `array<T>` to spread
   --> %s:16:18
   |
16 | mixed $any = [...$s];
   |                  ^^ this is `string`
   = help: `...` contributes the subject's own entries to the array being built, so the subject has to have entries — write the value as an ordinary element instead

error: aborting due to 3 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
